---

<Guide
  title="What the Bhagavad Gita says about death and grief"
  description="The Bhagavad Gita begins with a man grieving the deaths he fears he must cause. Its answer — verses 2.13, 2.20, 2.22 and 2.27 — is the heart of the whole text."
  slug="bhagavad-gita-on-death-and-grief"
  datePublished="2026-10-17"
  faq={[
    {
      q: 'What does the Bhagavad Gita say about death?',
      a: 'The Gita teaches that the body dies but the self (atman) does not — it is never born and never dies (verse 2.20). Death is a change of form, like changing worn-out clothes (2.22), so grief for the eternal self is misplaced even as grief for the body is natural.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Which Bhagavad Gita verse is about death?',
      a: 'The central verses are in Chapter 2: 2.13 (the self passes through childhood, youth and age, then to another body), 2.20 (the self is unborn and undying), 2.22 (changing bodies like worn garments), 2.23 (weapons cannot cut it), and 2.27 (death is certain for the born).',
    },
    {
      q: 'How does the Bhagavad Gita help with grief?',
      a: 'It does not deny grief — the whole text begins with Arjuna overcome by it. It reframes what was lost: the body ends, but the self you loved is not the body. It also counsels doing your duty through sorrow (2.27–2.28) rather than being paralysed by it.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Does the Bhagavad Gita believe in life after death?',
      a: 'Yes. It describes the self taking on a new body as a person puts on new clothes (2.22), and says the embodied self is eternal, passing through bodies rather than ending with them (2.13, 2.20).',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The Bhagavad Gita does not arrive at the subject of death gradually. It opens there. Before a
    single teaching is spoken, the warrior Arjuna looks across the battlefield at teachers, cousins
    and friends, and is undone by the deaths he believes he must cause. He sits down in his chariot
    and refuses to fight. Everything Krishna says afterward is, in one way or another, an answer to a
    grieving man.
  </p>
  <p>
    So if you have come here carrying a loss, you are reading the text at exactly the place it was
    written for. The Gita's reply is not "don't feel." It is a patient re-description of what dies and
    what does not.
  </p>

  <h2>The body changes; the one wearing it does not</h2>
  <p>
    Krishna's first move is to separate two things grief usually fuses together — the body, and the
    self that lived in it.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Just as the embodied self passes, in this body, through childhood, youth and age, so it passes to another body. The wise are not deluded by this."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/13')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.13</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    You have already watched a body change beyond recognition — the infant becomes the child becomes
    the elder — without the person being lost at each step. The Gita asks you to extend that same
    continuity one step further than the eye can follow.
  </p>

  <h2>What never dies</h2>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"The self is never born and never dies. It has not come into being, does not come into being, and will not come into being. Unborn, eternal, everlasting and ancient, it is not slain when the body is slain."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/20')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.20</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    This is the still centre of the Gita's consolation. The part of someone you actually loved — their
    awareness, the "I" that looked back at you — is, in the Gita's view, not the kind of thing that can
    end. The body it wore was always temporary; the one wearing it was not.
  </p>

  <h2>Like changing worn-out clothes</h2>
  <p>
    The Gita's most quoted image of death is almost gentle — domestic, even.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"As a person casts off worn-out garments and puts on new ones, so the embodied self casts off worn-out bodies and takes on others that are new."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/22')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.22</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    And then, to meet the specific fear that death is an undoing, a destruction of the person
    themselves:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"Weapons do not cut it, fire does not burn it, water does not wet it, wind does not wither it."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/23')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.23</a></cite>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Grief is natural — and so is going on</h2>
  <p>
    It would be a misreading to take all this as a command to feel nothing. The Gita is honest that
    death is certain and sorrow is human; what it resists is being frozen by it.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>"For the born, death is certain; for the dead, birth is certain. Over the unavoidable, then, you should not grieve."</p>
    <cite><a href={withBase('gita/2/27')}>Bhagavad Gita 2.27</a></cite>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Read coldly, that can sound dismissive. Read as counsel to someone unable to stand up, it is
    closer to a hand on the shoulder: this was always going to come; let it not take your own life
    along with it. The Gita's path through grief is not avoidance but movement — returning, gently, to
    what is still yours to do.
  </p>

  <h2>If the grief is heavy right now</h2>
  <p>
    Scripture can hold a loss, but it is not a substitute for people. If your grief feels unbearable or
    you are thinking of harming yourself, please reach out to someone you trust or a local crisis line
    — in India, iCall (9152987821) and KIRAN (1800-599-0019) are free and confidential. The verses
    will still be here afterward.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to read next</h2>
  <p>
    The whole of <a href={withBase('gita/2')}>Chapter 2</a> is the source of these verses and the
    tradition's "Gita in miniature." If grief is sitting alongside fear, the guide on
    <a href={withBase('guides/bhagavad-gita-on-fear')}>what the Gita says about fear</a> may help; if
    it has hardened into a heavier, flatter sadness, see
    <a href={withBase('guides/bhagavad-gita-on-depression')}>the Gita on despair and feeling low</a>.
    Or open Gita Daily and let a verse meet the feeling you have today.
  </p>
</Guide>
